65
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:33,280
things like poverty have changed over

66
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:35,720
time to get around this problem

67
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:37,920
governments tend to also publish data

68
00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:40,040
for a set of statistical areas that

69
00:02:40,040 --> 00:02:42,680
change much less often like the

70
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:44,680
administrative areas there are typically

71
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:46,879
several nested levels of statistical

72
00:02:46,879 --> 00:02:50,159
area in England for example statistical

73
00:02:50,159 --> 00:02:52,400
data is published for small areas called

74
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:55,000
output areas which are then grouped into

75
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,480
progressively larger areas with data

76
00:02:57,480 --> 00:02:59,480
sets being published for areas at each

77
00:02:59,480 --> 00:03:00,680
level

78
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:02,720
statistical data for local areas can

79
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:05,239
tell us a lot about these places but

80
00:03:05,239 --> 00:03:07,239
there is one problem we need to be aware

81
00:03:07,239 --> 00:03:10,799
of this is called the ecological fallacy

82
00:03:10,799 --> 00:03:12,920
the problem here is that when we look at

83
00:03:12,920 --> 00:03:15,760
maps that show data for different areas

84
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:17,760
our brains tend to assume that the data

85
00:03:17,760 --> 00:03:20,040
shown for a particular area applies

86
00:03:20,040 --> 00:03:22,799
equally to every street house or person

87
00:03:22,799 --> 00:03:25,680
in that area equally when there's a

88
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:28,280
difference between two areas on a map we

89
00:03:28,280 --> 00:03:29,799
tend to assume that the difference

90
00:03:29,799 --> 00:03:31,760
happens at the boundary between those

91
00:03:31,760 --> 00:03:34,360
areas this is a problem because in fact

92
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:36,319
it's quite likely that places just one

93
00:03:36,319 --> 00:03:38,640
side of the boundary or the other tend

94
00:03:38,640 --> 00:03:40,560
to be pretty similar to each other

95
00:03:40,560 --> 00:03:42,200
despite looking quite different on a

96
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:43,640
corle

97
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:46,120
map so whenever we look at a map that

98
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:48,760
shows data for areas we need to remember

99
00:03:48,760 --> 00:03:51,159
